Skribisto keeps your work safe in two different ways, and it helps to know which one you need.

A *backup* is a copy of the whole project, saved as a separate file. Use it when the project
file itself goes missing or is damaged.

*Version history* is the record of a single [scene, note or synopsis](:help-writing-model) as
you changed it. Use it to get back an earlier wording, or something you deleted.

## Backups

Skribisto makes backups on its own: when you close the project, and every two hours while you
work.

To make one now, choose *Work ▸ Back up now*.

To see what you already have, choose *Work ▸ Backups…*. The list shows every backup of this
project, and you can open, reveal or delete any of them.

### Where they go, and how many are kept

Backups go to a folder Skribisto looks after. You can add folders of your own.

Old backups are thinned out so they do not fill your disk. You choose how:

- *Tiered* keeps roughly one an hour for a day, one a day for a week, one a week for a month,
  and one a month after that.
- *Keep last N* keeps that many, newest first.

All of this lives in *Settings ▸ Backup & Sync ▸ Backup defaults*, and applies to every
project.

### Opening a backup

Opening a backup never touches the project you are working on. It opens in a window of its
own that you cannot save over, and a banner stays in view to remind you.

From that window you can:

- *Save As…* keeps the backup's contents as a separate project.
- *Restore…* puts the backup back in place of the original.

*Restore* is the only thing here that changes your real project. Before it does, it sets your
current project aside as a backup of its own, so nothing is lost either way.

## Version history

Version history follows one scene, note or synopsis at a time. It comes from two places, and
every entry tells you which one:

- *The project's own history* saves the text every time you do.
- *Backups* reach further back, however old they are.

### Looking back, and putting text back

The *Versions* panel sits on the right of the window, beside Comments and Format.

*Restore this version* puts the older text back. Skribisto takes a safety backup first, and
`Ctrl+Z` undoes it.

Pin a version that came from a backup and clearing out old files will never delete it.

### Something you deleted

*Restore this version* only works on something that is still there. For a scene or note you
deleted, choose *View ▸ Go back in time*.

*Bring this back…* recreates the item with the text it had, wherever in the binder you want it.
To undo that, use the button in the message that appears.
